; A loop driven entirely by you, written with `&` directly.
;
; Two ports, because they do two different jobs:
;
;   `more`  a gate. `%` needs exactly 0 or 1, and the port delivers it — so the
;           decision to go round again is yours and costs no model call.
;   `note`  a value. Used where a value flows (here as the arrow's producer) a
;           port hands over its text, so your instruction joins the work.
;
; This is the reason to write `&` yourself rather than use `std/seam`: that module
; fixes one port named `seam`, and a program needing two distinct inputs has to
; name them itself.
;
; Send `1` to keep working, then a note saying what to do next. Send `0` to stop
; and take the thread as it stands.
;
; MEASURED, two rounds: five deliveries from you (1, note, 1, note, 0) and three
; model calls. Note the ORDER — every delivery is taken before any model call
; runs. `%` is lazy and the thread only appears in the stopping branch, so the
; whole chain is assembled as source and evaluated once, at the end. That makes
; this a shape for DIRECTING work, not for reviewing it: you are saying what to
; do next, not judging what came back. To see each round before deciding, force
; the work into the gate and pay for the re-run — `examples/input.rebis` does
; exactly that, and costs nine calls for three rounds.
;
; With nothing wired, `more` is unbound, the gate sees neither 0 nor 1, and the
; run stops on an InvalidCondition having made no model call at all.
;
; Simpler still, if you only want to choose how many passes to run, one port is
; enough — drop `note` and let the prompt be fixed:
;
;   (~ keep-going (plan)
;     (& more (% more (keep-going (-> plan "Add the next step.")) plan)))

(~ session (thread)
  (& more
    (% more
      (& note
        (session
          (-> thread note "Carry the work on, following this note.")))
      thread)))

(session "Open a plan for tidying this project.")
